WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.020
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:11.139
Today we are going to simulate these absolutely
beautiful thin film structures.

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:15.950
You see, computer graphics researchers have
been writing physics simulation programs for

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:22.300
decades now, and the pace of progress in this
research area is absolutely stunning.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.740
Here are three examples of where we are at
the moment.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.950
One, this work was able to create a breathtaking
honey coiling simulation.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:36.641
I find it absolutely amazing that through
the power of computer graphics research, all

00:00:36.641 --> 00:00:40.170
this was possible four years ago.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.780
And the realistic simulation works just kept
coming in.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:49.859
This work appeared just one year ago and could
simulate not only a piece of viscous fluid,

00:00:49.859 --> 00:00:55.219
but, also deal with glugging and coalescing
bubbles.

00:00:55.219 --> 00:00:58.989
And three, this particular one is blazing
fast.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:05.680
So much so that it can simulate this dam break
scene in about 5 frames per second, not seconds

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.540
per frame, while it can run this water drop
scene with about about 7 frames per second.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:18.670
Remember, this simulates quantities like the
velocity, pressure, and more for several million

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.470
particles this quickly.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.700
Very impressive.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.310
So, are we done here?

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.370
Is there anything else left to be done in
fluid simulation research?

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:33.210
Well, hold on to your papers, and check this
out.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.430
This new paper can simulate thin-film phenomena.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.880
What does that mean?

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.730
Four things.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.950
First, here is a beautiful oscillating soap
bubble.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.640
Yes, its color varies as a function of the
evolving film thickness.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.810
But that’s not all.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:57.560
Let’s poke it, and then…did you see that?

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.770
It can even simulate it bursting into tiny,
sparkly droplets.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.250
Phew.

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.210
One more time.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.830
Loving it.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:14.859
Second, it can simulate one of my favorites,
the Rayleigh-Taylor instability.

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:21.230
The upper half of the thin film has a larger
density, while the lower half carries a larger

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.519
volume.

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:28.840
Essentially, this is the phenomenon when two
fluids of different densities meet.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.840
And what is the result?

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:31.840
Turbulence.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:39.150
First, the interface between the two is well
defined, but over time, it slowly disintegrates

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.959
into this beautiful swirly pattern.

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:44.859
Oh yeah…oh yeah!

00:02:44.859 --> 00:02:45.859
Look!

00:02:45.859 --> 00:02:48.519
And it just keeps on going and going.

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:54.519
Third, ah yes, the catenoid experiment.

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:56.349
What is that?

00:02:56.349 --> 00:03:01.709
This is a surface tension-driven deformation
experiment, where the film is trying to shrink

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:07.079
as we move the two rims away from each other,
forming this catenoid surface.

00:03:07.079 --> 00:03:12.900
Of course, we won’t stop there, what happens
when we keep moving them away?

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.140
What do you do think?

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.360
Please stop the video and let me know in the
comments below.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:18.749
I’ll wait.

00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:20.139
A little.

00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:21.139
Thank you!

00:03:21.139 --> 00:03:28.689
Now then, the membrane keeps shrinking, until…yes,
it finally collapses into a small droplet.

00:03:28.689 --> 00:03:34.799
The authors also went the extra mile and did
the most difficult thing for any physics simulation

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:38.510
paper…comparing the results to reality.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:45.829
So, is this just good enough to fool the untrained
human eye, or is this the real deal?

00:03:45.829 --> 00:03:52.889
Well, look at this, this is an actual photograph
of the catenoid experiment.

00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:55.480
And this is the simulation.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.069
Dear Fellow Scholars, that is a clean simulation
right there.

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:08.480
And, fourth, a thin film within a square subjected
to a gravitational pull that is changing over

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:09.650
time.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:14.379
And the result is more swirly patterns.

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:18.420
So how quickly can we perform all this?

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:23.800
Disregard the FPS, this is the inverse of
the time step size, and is mainly information

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.680
for fellow researchers.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.760
For now, gaze upon the time per frame column,
and, my goodness.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.970
This is blazing fast too!

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:39.240
It takes less than a second per frame for
the catenoid experiment, this is one of the

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.930
cheaper ones.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.280
And all this on a laptop!

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.340
Wow!

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:50.090
Now, the most expensive experiment in this
paper was the Rayleigh-Taylor instability,

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.889
this took about 13 seconds per frame.

00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:59.060
This is not bad at all, we can get a proper
simulation of this quality within an hour

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:00.470
or so.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:06.849
However, note that the authors used a big
honking machine to compute this scene.

00:05:06.849 --> 00:05:14.759
And remember, this paper is not about optimization,
but it is about making the impossible possible.

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:17.740
And it is doing all that, swiftly.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.979
Huge congratulations to the authors!

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:31.039
What 

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:51.979
a 

00:05:51.979 --> 00:06:11.009
time 
to be alive!

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:14.429
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

